# TODO - Tiered native execution engine

## Objective

Build a tiered execution engine instead of choosing between interpretation, AOT,
and JIT. Decode Malbolge into a compact execution IR, simplify that IR through
verified state-graph mathematics, compile demonstrably stable regions to native
machine code before execution, specialize hot or mutation-sensitive regions at
runtime, and deoptimize safely to the interpreter whenever a code-version guard
or speculative assumption fails. The normative VM contract remains the semantic
baseline; native tiers are accelerators of identical observable behavior.

## Acceptance

- Interpreter, AOT, JIT, native cache, and deoptimization share one observable
  VM contract; disabling every native tier produces the same guest behavior.
- Observable state, I/O, termination, and diagnostics match the declared
  semantic profile across positive, boundary, and adversarial fixtures.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `vm/`, `execution/`, `tests/vm/`,
  `benchmarks/interpreter/`.
- Required evidence: semantic fixtures, state/I/O traces where diagnostic, and
  differential results against independent specification-conformant
  implementations; the historical interpreter is compared only on its documented
  agreement domain.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `safe-rust-malbolge-vm`,
  `self-modification-state-graph-optimizer`.
- Active foundation: `execution/ir/main.rs` owns portable effect IR v3
  (`EffectOp`, `MemoryLiveIn`, `TargetProfileRequirement`, and
  `RegionEffectProgram`) without creating a Rust
  crate boundary. State-graph research reprojects verifier traces into this IR,
  rejects tampered profile-ID/fingerprint/version/features/geometry,
  live-in/budget/outcome/effect fields, and preserves
  deterministic interpreter deoptimization on guard miss.
- `execution/cache/main.rs` now binds canonical byte-exact IR identity to host
  OS, ISA, backend revision, native ABI revision, and required features. Digest
  collisions never authorize reuse because full canonical/key equality remains
  authoritative; `tests/tiered_execution.rs` forces that boundary explicitly.
- `execution/native/main.rs` now lowers structurally consistent effect IR to
  deterministic freestanding C23 under an exact `NativeArtifactKey`. All local
  entry/memory/input/output guards precede a collapsed final commit. Pinned
  Clang 22.1.8 emits real untrusted Windows COFF object candidates for both
  x86-64 and AArch64. Safe-Rust structural admission checks ISA, `.text`, entry
  identity, and relocation/symbol closure without trusting LLVM inspection.
  Direct `direct-*` objects must also carry exact read-only `.mbprof` metadata
  matching the profile ID/fingerprint, published version, semantic features,
  word trits, and capacity retained by their native key; absence or mismatch
  fails structurally. Bootstrap Clang objects do not yet emit it.
- A direct deopt-only backend now emits canonical x86-64/AArch64 COFF without
  Clang. Complete byte fixtures plus structural validation admit only the exact
  `return guard-miss` programs; opcode mutation fails semantic admission. This is
  the first semantically admitted native artifact and intentionally never applies
  effects.
- `direct-initial-halt` is the first admitted state-applying template. It accepts
  only zero-state/no-I/O/no-memory one-step halt IR, preflights before commit,
  changes only termination on hit, and returns guard miss atomically otherwise.
  Complete object fixtures and x86-64 hit/miss/null execution evidence cover the
  boundary; ARM64 object linkage is also verified.
- `direct-halt-registers` extends the same one-step halt contract across arbitrary
  32-bit `A/C/D` values while counters stay zero and memory/I/O effects remain
  forbidden. Full x86-64/AArch64 fixtures cover nontrivial immediates; x86-64 hit
  preserves registers and commits halt, while one-register miss is atomic.
- Direct-template orchestration now classifies IR before backend construction:
  exact zero-register halt selects the smallest fast path, other eligible halt
  IR selects the register-bound template, and remaining IR selects verified deopt. Unsupported host formats fail explicitly and
  post-selection errors never degrade silently to fallback.
- Accelerated semantic machine-code admission/direct effect emitters, AOT/JIT
  orchestration,
  durable cache storage/eviction, executable-memory policy, and end-to-end tier
  performance remain unimplemented.
